  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 10,
    "lesson_title": "Effect of $RC$ on Filter Behavior",
    "lesson_core_move": "Predict how changing $RC$ shifts the cutoff behavior and magnitude-response transition of RC low-pass and high-pass filters.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 10,
        "lesson_title": "Effect of $RC$ on Filter Behavior",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Effect of $RC$ on Filter Behavior",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you see an RC low-pass or high-pass filter, read the product $RC$ as the controlling quantity for cutoff location. The key idea is that changing $RC$ moves where the magnitude response turns.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_c = \\frac{1}{RC}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f_c = \\frac{1}{2\\pi RC}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $\\omega_c$ and $f_c$ are inversely proportional to $RC$, a larger $RC$ lowers the cutoff and a smaller $RC$ raises it. So the magnitude-response transition shifts to lower frequency when $RC$ increases and to higher frequency when $RC$ decreases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This same cutoff-setting rule applies to both RC low-pass and RC high-pass filters, so later examples can use one inverse relationship to predict how the transition moves in either response.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 10,
        "lesson_title": "Effect of $RC$ on Filter Behavior",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying $RC$ as the Cutoff-Setting Quantity",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Two RC low-pass filters have $(R,C) = (1\\,\\text{k}\\Omega, 1\\,\\mu\\text{F})$ and $(R,C) = (2\\,\\text{k}\\Omega, 0.5\\,\\mu\\text{F})$. Determine whether they have the same cutoff frequency and identify the controlling quantity.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the product $RC$: the cutoff depends on $RC$ itself, not on $R$ or $C$ alone. Compare the two filters by computing each product first.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nR_1C_1 &= (1\\,\\text{k}\\Omega)(1\\,\\mu\\text{F}) = 10^{-3}\\,\\text{s} \\\\\nR_2C_2 &= (2\\,\\text{k}\\Omega)(0.5\\,\\mu\\text{F}) = 10^{-3}\\,\\text{s}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the products match, substituting into $\\omega_c = 1/RC$ gives the same cutoff frequency for both filters. Equal $RC$ means equal cutoff location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the controlling quantity is $RC$. When $RC$ changes, the magnitude-response transition moves; when $RC$ stays the same, the transition stays in the same place.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 10,
        "lesson_title": "Effect of $RC$ on Filter Behavior",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Predicting a Low-Pass Magnitude Response After Changing $RC$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A low-pass filter has cutoff $\\omega_c = 1/RC$. If $RC$ doubles, determine the new cutoff frequency and state how the magnitude-response transition shifts relative to the original curve.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the inverse cutoff rule: for a low-pass RC filter, $RC$ sets the cutoff location. If $RC$ doubles, the new cutoff must move in the opposite direction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\omega_c' &= \\frac{1}{2RC} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{RC} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\omega_c}{2}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the new cutoff frequency is half the original cutoff frequency. The magnitude-response transition shifts to lower frequency, which means the bend in the low-pass curve moves left on the frequency axis.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The controlling quantity is still $RC$: changing $R$ or $C$ matters only through their product. When that product gets larger, the cutoff gets smaller and the transition moves left; when the product gets smaller, the cutoff gets larger and the transition moves right.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 10,
        "lesson_title": "Effect of $RC$ on Filter Behavior",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Predicting a High-Pass Magnitude Response After Changing $RC$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A high-pass filter has cutoff $\\omega_c = 1/RC$. If $RC$ doubles, determine the new cutoff frequency and say whether a frequency just below the original cutoff is closer to or farther from the passband.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same inverse cutoff rule, but now we read it on a high-pass curve. For a high-pass RC filter, larger $RC$ still lowers the cutoff, so the transition moves to a lower frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\omega_c' &= \\frac{1}{2RC} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{RC} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\omega_c}{2}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the new cutoff frequency is half the original cutoff frequency. A frequency just below the original cutoff is now above the new cutoff, so it is closer to the passband and less attenuated than before.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The controlling quantity is still $RC$: changing $R$ or $C$ matters only through their product. The rule is the same for both RC filter types; the only difference is which side of the cutoff counts as the passband.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 10,
        "lesson_title": "Effect of $RC$ on Filter Behavior",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Comparing Magnitude-Response Transitions for Different $RC$ Values",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Two RC filters satisfy $RC_1 < RC_2$. Compare their cutoff frequencies and magnitude-response transition locations for both the low-pass and high-pass responses.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the ordering of the two $RC$ values. Since $\\omega_c = 1/RC$, the larger product gives the lower cutoff frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n\\omega_{c,1} &= \\frac{1}{RC_1} \\\\\n\\omega_{c,2} &= \\frac{1}{RC_2} \\\\\nRC_1 < RC_2 &\\Rightarrow \\omega_{c,1} > \\omega_{c,2}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the filter with $RC_2$ has the lower cutoff frequency. For the low-pass response, its magnitude transition occurs at the lower frequency, so that curve is shifted farther left than the curve with $RC_1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same ordering holds for the high-pass response. The filter with $RC_2$ begins its transition at the lower frequency, so its rising edge is also shifted farther left.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The shared conclusion is that larger $RC$ lowers the cutoff and moves the magnitude-response transition to lower frequency for both RC filter types. The low-pass and high-pass curves differ in shape, but they follow the same cutoff-ordering rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
